                'content' => "The best data stewards do not wait for an audit; they audit themselves, and the reason is simple. A monthly self-audit catches the small errors that become the big rejections, and it takes ten minutes if you know what to check. The audit is not a test of the registry; it is a test of your own record, and the grower who passes their own audit passes the field agent's visit without a surprise.\n\nStart with identity: does the name on your registry profile match your identity document exactly, including middle names, initials, and spelling? Then check the farm location: does the pin sit inside the boundary, and does the stand count match what you can see from the edge of the estate? These two checks catch the most common registry errors, and both take two minutes each. A name that differs by one letter is the difference between a confirmed record and a returned application.\n\nNext, walk the documents. Are the scans current, readable, and correctly named? Does every active program you rely on have the document it requires? A document that expired last month is a problem this month, and the audit is where you find it before the verifier does. Open each document in your profile the way a reviewer would, and ask the question the reviewer asks: is this current, is this legible, and does this match the name and the farm on the rest of the profile?\n\nThen read your activity for gaps. Are your task submissions complete, and do your records show the work you actually did? The audit is not a test of perfection; it is a test of honesty, and the honest record with one noted gap beats the perfect record with three hidden ones. Write the gaps down as you find them, because a gap you have written is a gap you will fix, and a gap you have noticed but ignored is a gap the verifier will notice for you.\n\nFinish by writing down what you fixed and what you will fix next month. The audit habit compounds: the first month you find five errors, the third month you find one, and the sixth month you find none. The grower with clean records is the grower who passes verification on the first visit, every time, and the grower who audits monthly is the grower whose records survive any inspection without a scramble.\n\nThe habit is the point. Ten minutes a month is an hour a year, and the hour a year is what keeps the profile verified, the programs open, and the market reachable. The grower who builds the audit habit builds the record that works for them in every season that follows.",
