                'summary' => 'Plan the work around the sun, not the other way round.',
                'duration' => 30,
                'content' => "Coconut farms are hot, and heat illness is the silent hazard that sends strong workers to hospital every season. The defense is not toughness; it is planning, and the farm that plans the work around the sun keeps every worker while the farm that lets the sun plan the work loses one. Start the day early, do the heaviest work before ten in the morning, and schedule the shade breaks the way you schedule the tasks, because a break that is planned is a break that is taken.\n\nHydration is a rhythm, not a thirst response. Drink a cup of water every hour whether you feel thirsty or not, because by the time you feel thirsty you are already behind, and the worker who waits for thirst is the worker who arrives at noon already dehydrated. Keep water at the work site, in a clean container, and never share a cup. A worker who drinks small amounts often stays stronger through the afternoon than one who gulps a large amount at noon and then works on empty.\n\nRest in shade, not in the sun. A ten-minute rest in the shade every hour is worth an hour of struggling through the heat, and the shade that is planned is the shade that is used. Watch your crew for the warning signs: confusion, nausea, a headache, a stop in sweating, or skin that is hot and dry. Those are the signs of heat stroke, and they mean stop work immediately, move the person to shade, cool them with water and fanning, and call for help. The worker who is cooled quickly recovers; the worker who is left in the sun does not.\n\nDress for the heat: a hat, light clothing, and a shirt that breathes, and change out of wet clothing when it cools the body too much in the wind. Use a buddy system on the hottest days, and never let a new worker prove themselves in the midday sun, because the new worker is the one who does not yet know the signs in their own body. The experienced hand who checks on the new hand is the farm's cheapest safety equipment.\n\nFinally, plan the work itself for the heat: climb in the cool hours, do the precision work before the heat peaks, and leave the heavy lifting for the early morning. The farm that plans around the sun keeps every worker, and the worker who plans for heat never has to recover from it. The season is long, and the worker who finishes it healthy is the worker who is still there for the next one.",
