                'content' => "A clear price is a promise, and the provider who prices clearly sells faster than the provider who hides the number behind a conversation. Clarity has three parts: the unit, the inclusions, and the terms, and the price that names all three is the price that wins the order without an argument. This lesson shows how to build that clarity into every quote, listing, and invoice you send.\n\nStart with the unit. Price per hectare, per thousand palms, per day, or per job, and say which. A price without a unit is a riddle, and the buyer who cannot compare your price to another will not buy from you, because comparison is how buyers decide. Write the unit in your listing, in your quotes, and in every invoice, and never leave the buyer to guess whether your number is for a day, a hectare, or a whole contract.\n\nNext, state the inclusions. Does the price cover travel, materials, supervision, and a follow-up visit, or is it the labor only? The provider who writes what is included stops the disputes before they start, and the provider who leaves it to the imagination collects the argument at the end of the job. If something is excluded, say so plainly: the excluded item named today is not an argument tomorrow, and the exclusion written down is the exclusion the buyer accepted.\n\nThen set the terms: when payment is due, what cancels the service, and what happens if the work is not completed on schedule. The terms protect both sides, because the provider who knows when they will be paid can plan the work, and the buyer who knows what cancels the service knows what they are committing to. The provider who prices with terms is the provider who gets paid, and the provider who prices without terms is the provider who chases.\n\nFinally, review your price quarterly against your costs and your completion rate. A price that never moves while costs rise is a loss, and a price that moves without a reason is a mystery to the buyer. Keep a simple record of what each job cost you in time and materials, and price the next job from that record rather than from memory. The clear, current, costed price is the one that builds the referral business, and the referral is the cheapest customer you will ever win, because it arrives already trusting you.",
