                'content' => "The first three listings set the pattern of your store, so choose them with the buyer in mind rather than with the whole harvest in mind. The pattern that works is simple: one hero product, one everyday product, and one seasonal product. The hero shows your best grade, the everyday product shows your volume, and the seasonal product shows your range, and together they tell the buyer what your store is for.\n\nFor the hero listing, pick the grade you can supply every week at a consistent quality, photograph it in good light with a clean background, and write the grade, the count, and the packing standard in the description. The hero listing is the one buyers remember, so make it accurate rather than ambitious, because the listing that promises the moon and delivers a stone earns the review that follows you into every later listing. The grade you can hold every week is the grade to lead with.\n\nFor the everyday listing, choose the product that moves the most volume at a fair price. Keep the price honest, the stock number real, and the delivery window true, because buyers forgive a small price but they do not forgive a stock number that was never there. The everyday listing is the one that builds the repeat business, and the repeat business is the store's real income, so the listing that is always accurate is the listing that always sells.\n\nFor the seasonal listing, add the product that only comes in season, and mark the months clearly so the buyer who plans ahead can find it. The seasonal listing shows the buyer you have range and planning, and it brings back the buyers who buy early to secure the supply. A seasonal listing with clear months is a promise kept, and a seasonal listing without them is a surprise the buyer does not come back for.\n\nFinally, keep all three listings current: update the stock weekly, refresh the photos each season, and respond to every inquiry within the day. Three honest listings, kept current, beat ten stale ones, and the store that is current is the store that sells, because the buyer who finds what they want, at the stock you have, at the price you wrote, is the buyer who orders today and returns next season.",
