                'content' => "The knife is the farm hand's most used tool and the cause of more lost fingers than any machine on a coconut estate, which is why the rules for it are simple and why the hand that follows them keeps all ten. The rules are few, but they are absolute, and the moment you break one is the moment the blade finds the hand it was meant to be pointed away from.\n\nFirst, cut away from the body and never toward it, and never cut toward a hand, a leg, or a co-worker standing nearby. When you cut a frond, a rope, or a husk, the blade goes where you point it, so point it where the waste falls, not where the worker stands, because a blade that slips goes where it was aimed. The cut that starts away from the body ends without a story, and the story-free day is the day the crew goes home whole.\n\nSecond, keep the blade sharp and the tool right for the job. A blunt knife slips and a slipping knife cuts the hand, so sharpen it before it needs force rather than after it needs luck. Use a cutlass for the fronds and a knife for the husks, and never use a blade as a lever, a screwdriver, or a chisel, because the blade used as a tool it was not made to be is the blade that breaks or bends into the hand. The tool used for its own job stays whole, and the whole tool is the safe tool.\n\nThird, carry tools closed and sheathed, never in a pocket and never loose in a bag where they can find the hand that reaches in, and hand them over handle-first so the receiver never takes the blade. When you set a tool down, set it where it cannot roll and where it cannot be stepped on, and never leave a blade on the ground where it can be grabbed by mistake in the rush of the afternoon. The tool put down safely is the tool picked up safely.\n\nFinally, treat every cut immediately: clean water, pressure, and a clean dressing, and report it to your supervisor even when it is small, because the small cut reported today is the one that heals cleanly and the small cut hidden is the one that infects. The hand that heals is the hand that works the next season, and the farm that treats its cuts well keeps its crew.",
