                'content' => "Seedlings live or die on water and shade, and the nursery that manages both deliberately grows the seedlings that the field buys, while the nursery that leaves both to chance grows the losses the field refuses. The two go together: shade cools the seedling and slows the water, and water replaces what the shade saves, so you manage them as one system rather than two separate chores.\n\nStart with the watering rhythm. Seedlings in small containers dry within a day in hot weather, so water daily in the dry season and twice daily at the peak of the heat, and water at the base rather than over the fronds, because the roots drink and the leaves only get wet. Water enough that the root zone is wet through without standing water at the bottom of the pot, which rots the roots. The seedling watered at the roots grows; the seedling sprinkled on the leaves wilts by afternoon.\n\nNext, manage the shade. Young seedlings need half to three-quarters shade for the first months, then gradual hardening before field planting, because the seedling that has never seen full sun cannot survive it on the day it is moved. Use shade cloth, palm fronds, or a nurse crop, and increase the light slowly over weeks so the seedling builds the fronds and the cuticle it will need in the open. The seedling moved from shade to full sun in one day is the seedling that scorches, and the scorched seedling is the seedling that dies.\n\nWatch the signs daily: pale fronds can mean too much water, folded fronds can mean too little, and scorched tips mean too much sun. The operator who reads these signs catches the problem in the morning and corrects it by noon, and the operator who does not read them finds the nursery already damaged by the weekend. Walk the beds every morning before the heat, because the morning is when the signs are clearest and the corrections are cheapest.\n\nFinally, group the seedlings by age and need, so the youngest get the most shade and the oldest get the most light, and the ones in between get the transition. The nursery that stages shade and water by age grows uniform, field-ready seedlings, and the uniform seedling is the one that sells first, because the buyer who plants a uniform lot harvests a uniform stand, and the uniform stand is the profitable one.",
