                'content' => "The season review is where the year's records become next year's decisions, and the grower who holds it every season improves the farm while the grower who skips it repeats the same mistakes at the same cost. The meeting is simple: one hour, the records, and three questions, and the grower who asks all three and writes the answers owns the improvement rather than hoping for it.\n\nThe first question is what happened. Read the yield log by block, the input log, and the labor record, and write the headline numbers: total yield, cost per hectare, and the best and worst blocks. The numbers on paper are the facts of the season, and the facts are what the review works from, so write them before you explain them. The grower who starts with the numbers cannot hide a bad block behind a good story, and cannot credit a good block to luck rather than to the practice that caused it.\n\nThe second question is why. For the best block, what caused the result: the variety, the nutrition, the pest control, the water, or the age of the stand? For the worst block, what failed, and is the cause fixable or only survivable? The grower who finds the cause finds the action, and the grower who skips the why repeats the what, because a result without a cause cannot be repeated on purpose, and a failure without a cause cannot be prevented on purpose.\n\nThe third question is what changes. Choose three decisions only, so the plan is small enough to keep: the block to rehabilitate, the input to change, and the practice to start or stop. Write them with dates and owners, even when the owner is only the grower, because a decision that is written and dated is a decision that is done, and a decision that lives only in memory is a decision that is forgotten by the time the season turns.\n\nFinally, file the review with the records and set the next review date before you close the book. The season review that is written down is the plan that gets done, and the grower who reviews every season is the grower whose farm improves every season, because the improvement is not a single decision but the habit of deciding, and the habit of deciding is what separates the farm that compounds its gains from the farm that only repeats its years.\n\nDo not wait for a good season to hold the review, or for a bad one to make it feel urgent. The review that happens every season, whatever the weather, is the review that catches the small changes before they become the big losses, and it is also the review that notices the practices worth repeating before they are forgotten. Bring the records, bring the three questions, and bring a pen, because the answer you write down is the answer that survives until the next season. The farm that reviews is the farm that remembers, the farm that remembers is the farm that improves, and the farm that improves is the farm that is still profitable when the years turn against the farms that only repeated.",
