                'summary' => 'Know the refund window and the escalation path.',
                'duration' => 30,
                'content' => "Refunds are the part of payments every learner hopes to ignore and every learner should understand, because the rules protect the learner before the problem arrives rather than after it has grown. Three things matter: the window, the conditions, and the path, and the learner who knows all three knows whether their case is a refund, a conversation, or a closed door.\n\nThe window is the time you have to request a refund, and it runs from the payment date, not from the day you notice the problem, which is why the policy is worth reading when you enroll rather than when you need it. The refund requested after the window closes is the refund that cannot be granted, however good the reason, because the window is the promise both sides made, and the promise that is kept is the promise that still stands. Mark the deadline in your calendar the day you pay.\n\nThe conditions are what the refund covers: usually a course that was not delivered, a payment taken twice, or a service not received, and usually not a course the learner completed, a certificate already issued, or a payment the learner authorized and used. The learner who knows the conditions knows whether their case is a refund or a conversation, and the learner who assumes everything is refundable is the learner who is surprised when the answer is no. Read the conditions before you pay, so the purchase is the purchase you intended.\n\nThe path is the escalation order: the support desk first, with the transaction reference and the evidence; then the platform's dispute process if the desk cannot resolve it; and then the payment provider's chargeback route as the last resort, because the chargeback is the tool that costs the most trust and should be the one you reach for last. The case that is escalated in order is the case that is resolved quickly, and the case that jumps the queue is the case that stalls, because every handler needs the record the earlier step was meant to build.\n\nFinally, keep the records: the receipt, the reference, the messages, and the dates. The dispute with evidence is the dispute that is settled in the learner's favor, and the learner who keeps records keeps the refund, because the case that can be shown is the case that can be judged, and the case that can be judged is the case that is decided on the facts rather than on the memory.\n\nMost payment problems are small, and most are solved at the first step, and the learner who starts at the support desk with the transaction reference and the evidence usually gets the answer within a day. The rules are not there to make refunds hard; they are there to make them fair, so the honest learner with a real problem is the one the process is built to help. Read the window, the conditions, and the path when you enroll, keep the receipt and the reference in a safe place, and the whole subject stays quiet in the corner of your mind where it belongs. The learner who knows the rules before the trouble is the learner the rules protect, and the protection is worth more than any single refund.",
            ],
        ],
    ],
    152 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Quality Standards and Grading',
                'summary' => 'Buy by grade, so the grade is what you get.',
                'duration' => 30,
                'content' => "The buyer who buys by grade gets the grade, and the buyer who buys by description gets a surprise, which is why grading is the shared language of the marketplace and why the procurement professional who knows the grades knows what to order and what to pay. A grade is not a label; it is a set of measurable traits, and the buyer who learns those traits can order them, inspect for them, and hold the supplier to them.\n\nStart with the grade definitions. Every product on the platform is graded by measurable traits: size, moisture, damage, and age, and the grades for the products you buy are written in the product standards. Learn the grades so the order you place names the grade you want and the price you expect, because the order that names the grade is the order that arrives as specified, and the order that names only a description is the order that arrives as the seller decided. The named grade is the contract.\n\nNext, inspect before you accept. Check the delivery against the grade: count the units, check the quality against the traits the grade promises, and note the condition at the door. The inspection at the door is the buyer's protection, and the buyer who accepts without inspecting pays for the seller's worst lot and calls it the price of doing business. The inspection that takes five minutes at the door saves the dispute that takes weeks to unwind, and it is the only moment you can still reject the lot.\n\nThen tie the price to the grade. The grade one lot earns the grade one price, and a lot that arrives below the ordered grade earns a renegotiation, not the full price, because the buyer who ties price to grade never overpays for under-grade produce and never underpays for the grade they actually received. The price tied to the grade is the price both sides agreed to, and the price that is tied is the price that is defensible in a dispute and fair in a relationship.\n\nFinally, record the grade and the inspection on the order. The record is the evidence for a dispute and the history for the next order, and the buyer who keeps the grading history builds the supplier list that delivers the grade, at the price, every season, because the supplier who is held to the grade learns to deliver it, and the buyer who keeps the record is the buyer who can prove the standard the supplier agreed to.\n\nGrading only works when both sides honor it: the seller who grades honestly and the buyer who inspects fairly. The standard is the meeting point of the two, and the record is the proof that the meeting happened, because a grade without a record is a claim and a record without an inspection is a form. The procurement professional who learns the grades, inspects at the door, ties the price to what arrived, and keeps the history turns a risky purchase into a reliable supply. A reliable supply is the whole point of buying by grade, and the buyer who builds that reliability is the buyer whose own reputation grows with every order they place and every grade they honor.",
            ],
