    203 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Your First Season as a Provider',
                'summary' => 'From approval to first paid service.',
                'duration' => 30,
                'content' => "Approval as an input or service provider is the start of the business, not the end of the paperwork, and the provider who plans the first season turns the approval into income while the provider who waits for the phone to ring turns it into a fee they never recover. The season has four stages: listing, quoting, delivering, and following up, and the provider who works all four turns one approval into the repeat business that funds the second season.\n\nListing comes first. Write the services you will actually deliver, the areas you will actually cover, and the prices you will actually hold, because a short, honest list of two services beats a long, hopeful list of ten, and the service you deliver well is the service that earns the review that brings the next customer. The listing that names the real coverage area is the listing that gets the inquiries you can actually answer, and the inquiry you can answer is the order you can win.\n\nQuoting is the conversation. When a grower asks, quote the unit, the inclusions, and the delivery window within the day, because the quote that arrives first and names the price clearly is the quote that wins, and the quote that is vague is the quote that loses the order to a clearer competitor. The quote that is honest about what is included is the quote that does not become an argument at the end of the job, and the provider who quotes clearly is the provider who is chosen again.\n\nDelivering is the promise kept. Show up on the scheduled day, do the work to the standard you quoted, and leave the grower with the record of what was done, because the service delivered as quoted is the service that is paid, and the payment that is paid is the business that continues. The grower who can see the work done to the standard promised is the grower who asks for you again, and the ask that is repeated is the income that is stable.\n\nFinally, follow up: ask the grower to confirm the work, request the review, and note the date for the next visit. The provider who follows up turns one order into the season's repeat orders, and the repeat order is the foundation of a provider business that survives the first season and grows into the second, because the business that is built on follow-up is built on trust, and the trust that is followed up is the trust that returns.\n\nThe four stages are simple, but they are also the whole business: list what you can do, quote it clearly, deliver it as promised, and follow up. The provider who does these four things well in the first season does not need luck, because the grower who was served well becomes the customer who returns, and the customer who returns is the income that carries the provider through every season that follows. The approval you received was permission to start; these four stages are the work that makes the permission profitable. Do them deliberately, write down what each job cost, and price the next one from the record, and the first season becomes the foundation of a business rather than a year of hoping the phone would ring.",
            ],
        ],
    ],
